366
00:32:03,823 --> 00:32:08,225
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, when craving is present, acquisition is.

367
00:32:09,285 --> 00:32:13,426
[SPEAKER_01]: When acquisition is present, manifold suffering in the world is.

368
00:32:15,167 --> 00:32:19,949
[SPEAKER_01]: If it comes to be, you kind of expect a certain amount of time to pass, but no.

369
00:32:20,309 --> 00:32:23,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of craving equals presence of acquisition.

370
00:32:23,850 --> 00:32:26,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving, absence of acquisition.

371
00:32:26,625 --> 00:32:36,947
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, simply by nurturing that upon which craving depends, you nurture death and everything else, at the very other end of the same principle.

372
00:32:38,727 --> 00:32:52,750
[SPEAKER_02]: As it says, with regard... if they regard the world with a pleasant... as a pleasant and agreeable nature, they regard it as permanent and happiness itself, as healthy, as secure, then there's nurturing of craving.

373
00:32:53,843 --> 00:33:00,365
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to go around and regard everything as horrible, despicable.

374
00:33:00,846 --> 00:33:05,847
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is just regard the pleasure as such, when it arises.

375
00:33:06,428 --> 00:33:15,091
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is no pleasure, no need for anything, no need for any application of that medicine I was talking about before.

376
00:33:16,651 --> 00:33:22,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when it's a neutral feeling, all you need to do is understand it, not engage in indolence.

377
00:33:22,908 --> 00:33:24,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Do not engage in distraction.

378
00:33:24,991 --> 00:33:26,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Do not run away from all of this.

379
00:33:45,530 --> 00:33:50,151
[SPEAKER_01]: But it might sound like, oh, world is pain, world is a dart.

380
00:33:50,631 --> 00:33:51,211
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, yes.

381
00:33:51,391 --> 00:33:51,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

382
00:33:51,671 --> 00:33:53,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you regard world as pleasurable.

383
00:33:54,672 --> 00:34:04,134
[SPEAKER_01]: But you apply the notion of this is pain, this is pain, this is dart, only to that pleasure in regard to the world.

384
00:34:05,075 --> 00:34:09,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do so, there won't be any craving in regard to the world.

385
00:34:09,036 --> 00:34:12,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of craving, there won't be any acquisitions in the world.

386
00:34:12,636 --> 00:34:14,897
[SPEAKER_01]: No acquisitions, no suffering.

387
00:34:16,422 --> 00:34:17,022
[SPEAKER_01]: No suffering.

388
00:34:18,243 --> 00:34:18,703
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

389
00:34:26,188 --> 00:34:27,069
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he gives a simile.

390
00:34:28,830 --> 00:34:34,834
[SPEAKER_02]: Suppose, Bhikkhus, there was a bronze cup of a beverage having a fine color, aroma and taste.

391
00:34:35,594 --> 00:34:36,835
[SPEAKER_02]: But it was mixed with poison.

392
00:34:37,516 --> 00:34:42,539
[SPEAKER_02]: Then a man would come along, oppressed and afflicted by the heat, tired, parched and thirsty.

393
00:34:43,310 --> 00:34:48,234
[SPEAKER_02]: They would tell you, good man, this beverage in the bronze cup has a fine color, aroma and taste.

394
00:34:49,015 --> 00:34:50,896
[SPEAKER_02]: But it is mixed with poison.

395
00:34:50,916 --> 00:34:51,677
[SPEAKER_02]: Drink it if you wish.

396
00:34:52,697 --> 00:34:56,541
[SPEAKER_02]: If you drink it, it will gratify you with its color, aroma and taste.

397
00:34:57,101 --> 00:35:01,064
[SPEAKER_02]: But by drinking it, you will meet with... with death or deadly suffering.

398
00:35:02,205 --> 00:35:04,687
[SPEAKER_02]: Suddenly, without reflecting, he would drink the beverage.

399
00:35:05,688 --> 00:35:09,551
[SPEAKER_02]: He would not reject it, and thereby, he would meet death or deadly suffering.

400
00:35:10,425 --> 00:35:12,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can think of the same simile.

401
00:35:12,426 --> 00:35:17,128
[SPEAKER_01]: In example, imagine I come to you and I actually give you poison.

402
00:35:18,729 --> 00:35:20,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I say, it tastes nice.

403
00:35:21,391 --> 00:35:22,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you drink it?

404
00:35:22,731 --> 00:35:23,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you drink any of it?

405
00:35:24,632 --> 00:35:25,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I tell you, it's a deadly poison.

406
00:35:26,073 --> 00:35:26,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Here it is.

407
00:35:26,853 --> 00:35:27,994
[SPEAKER_01]: This bottle has a poison.

408
00:35:28,054 --> 00:35:28,814
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a poison.

409
00:35:29,615 --> 00:35:30,535
[SPEAKER_01]: 100% poison.

410
00:35:31,716 --> 00:35:33,176
[SPEAKER_01]: It has a very nice taste, apparently.

411
00:35:33,977 --> 00:35:34,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you like...

412
00:35:34,657 --> 00:35:35,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Here you go.

413
00:35:35,097 --> 00:35:35,878
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you like to drink it?

414
00:35:36,218 --> 00:35:37,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Only if I was there.

415
00:35:38,139 --> 00:35:40,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, only if you haven't heard me, then I say it's poison.

416
00:35:40,722 --> 00:35:46,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, it's, you know, oh, this is a lovely drink, then you have to discern it's actually a poison.

417
00:35:46,765 --> 00:35:49,627
[SPEAKER_01]: But imagine if somebody comes in, there's no lovely drink, it's straight poison.

418
00:36:10,986 --> 00:36:13,307
[SPEAKER_01]: That tastes lovely, like a lovely drink.

419
00:36:14,367 --> 00:36:17,088
[SPEAKER_01]: But you wouldn't even consider it as a lovely drink, i.e.

420
00:36:17,128 --> 00:36:17,648
[SPEAKER_01]: drinkable.

421
00:36:18,069 --> 00:36:18,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

422
00:36:18,349 --> 00:36:19,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's a poison.

423
00:36:19,509 --> 00:36:20,390
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be worth it.

424
00:36:20,410 --> 00:36:21,290
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be valuable.

425
00:36:21,630 --> 00:36:25,912
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just it will not have the significance of to be drank, because it's a poison.

426
00:36:27,492 --> 00:36:32,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when you see a a cup with a nice nice drink, nice juice, cocktail, whatever.

427
00:36:33,074 --> 00:36:35,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is this is a drink.

428
00:36:35,175 --> 00:36:37,376
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm gonna drink it, but this is a drinkable.

429
00:36:38,357 --> 00:36:48,460
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you see a bottle with the scarlet bones on it, that's equally, probably even tastier than this drink, you still won't regard it as drinkable, because it's a poison.

430
00:36:50,461 --> 00:36:51,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's my point here.

431
00:36:52,701 --> 00:37:01,084
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you actually need to start overriding that which you regard as drinkable, as consumable, as delightful, as agreeable, as pleasurable.

432
00:37:01,264 --> 00:37:01,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality.

433
00:37:02,604 --> 00:37:05,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, not even as such, because it's a poison.

434
00:37:14,537 --> 00:37:30,324
[SPEAKER_02]: Because whatever ascetics and brahmins in the past, the future or present, regarded that in their world with a pleasant and agreeable nature, as impermanent, as suffering, as non-self, as a disease, as fearful, they abandoned craving.

435
00:37:31,364 --> 00:37:33,585
[SPEAKER_02]: In abandoning craving, they abandoned acquisition.

436
00:37:33,945 --> 00:37:36,767
[SPEAKER_02]: In abandoning acquisition, they abandoned suffering.

437
00:37:36,787 --> 00:37:37,327
[SPEAKER_02]: In abandoning

438
00:37:38,273 --> 00:37:40,994
[SPEAKER_02]: In abandoning suffering, they were freed from birth, aging and death.

439
00:37:41,254 --> 00:37:45,095
[SPEAKER_02]: They were freed from sorrow, lamentation, pain, displeasure and despair.

440
00:37:45,755 --> 00:37:46,855
[SPEAKER_02]: They were freed from suffering.

441
00:37:48,656 --> 00:38:02,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, just to reiterate, as it says there in the beginning, that which you... that which is pleasurable, they regard it as pain, as fear, as impermanent, as dangerous, as disease.

442
00:38:02,579 --> 00:38:04,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Not everything around you.

443
00:38:04,499 --> 00:38:06,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Because things that are neutral, things that are

444
00:38:07,608 --> 00:38:13,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Irrelevant in terms of sensuality, in terms of pleasure, in terms of delight, in terms of attraction, you don't have to worry about.

445
00:38:20,534 --> 00:38:22,154
[SPEAKER_03]: And he gives another simile.

446
00:38:22,835 --> 00:38:28,557
[SPEAKER_02]: Suppose because there was a bronze cup of a beverage having a fine color, aroma and taste, but it was mixed with poison.

447
00:38:29,337 --> 00:38:33,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Then a man would come along, oppressed and afflicted by the heat, tired, parched and thirsty.

448
00:38:34,800 --> 00:38:35,400
[SPEAKER_02]: They would tell him,

449
00:38:36,252 --> 00:38:41,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Good man, this beverage in the bronze cup has a fine color, aroma and taste, but it is mixed with poison.

450
00:38:42,216 --> 00:38:43,196
[SPEAKER_02]: Drink it if you wish.

451
00:38:44,097 --> 00:38:47,419
[SPEAKER_02]: If you drink it, it will gratify you with its color, aroma and taste.

452
00:38:47,819 --> 00:38:51,581
[SPEAKER_02]: But by drinking it, you will meet death or deadly suffering.

453
00:38:52,342 --> 00:38:58,105
[SPEAKER_02]: Then the man would think, I can quench my thirst with water, whey, porridge or soup.

454
00:38:59,426 --> 00:39:05,310
[SPEAKER_02]: But I should not drink that beverage, since to do so would lead to my harm and suffering for a long time.

455
00:39:05,753 --> 00:39:08,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the point I just said.

456
00:39:08,815 --> 00:39:15,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Having reflected, he would not drink that beverage, but would reject it, and thereby he would not meet death.

457
00:39:16,080 --> 00:39:22,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that are not pleasurable, inciting, delightful, intoxicating, they're not a problem.

458
00:39:23,465 --> 00:39:28,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he can drink water where things that are not desirable, but serve the purpose.

459
00:39:29,809 --> 00:39:33,152
[SPEAKER_01]: But things that are desirable are the ones where sensuality is.

460
00:39:34,572 --> 00:39:35,253
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically,

461
00:39:35,686 --> 00:39:40,047
[SPEAKER_01]: By freeing yourself from that, by seeing the danger in desire, you free yourself from desire.

462
00:39:40,487 --> 00:39:45,648
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you stop eating, you stop walking, you stop dressing, you stop talking, you stop doing whatever you were doing.

463
00:39:46,868 --> 00:39:49,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless, obviously, whatever you were doing was unwholesome, based on sensuality.

464
00:39:49,868 --> 00:39:50,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you won't be doing that.

465
00:39:52,369 --> 00:39:54,189
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's exactly what that man realizes.

466
00:39:54,629 --> 00:40:02,811
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that sweet tip of the pleasure that he has to say no, because that's where the poison is.

467
00:40:02,851 --> 00:40:04,651
[SPEAKER_01]: All he has to do is drink water.

468
00:40:05,430 --> 00:40:11,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Bring something else, that it's not sensuality, that it's not craved after, that it's not delighted in.

469
00:40:11,851 --> 00:40:13,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Something that is neutral.

470
00:40:29,134 --> 00:40:31,495
[SPEAKER_03]: Saṅgha doesn't give rise to desire.

471
00:40:34,391 --> 00:40:37,753
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can only do that when you fully understand the danger of desire.

472
00:40:38,253 --> 00:40:42,436
[SPEAKER_01]: When you can spend your life eating bland food and so on, and still be bound by sensuality.

473
00:40:43,396 --> 00:40:46,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, obviously, you're not seeing that desire is dangerous.

474
00:40:47,599 --> 00:40:50,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just trying to avoid it, prevent it from arising.

475
00:41:03,072 --> 00:41:16,323
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to prevent it from arising, once and for all, is to develop full dispassion in regards to it, in regard to it, not by trying to avoid things.

476
00:41:16,623 --> 00:41:18,144
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to start by avoiding, i.e.

477
00:41:18,164 --> 00:41:21,807
[SPEAKER_01]: by restraining, but that in itself will not lead to understanding.

478
00:41:23,709 --> 00:41:31,235
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I said before, it's a necessary prerequisite, offers a basis for developing understanding, but in itself avoiding

479
00:41:31,988 --> 00:41:37,674
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, still avoiding sensuality is better than engaging in sensuality, but avoiding the self will not lead to the end of sensuality.

480
00:41:38,255 --> 00:41:46,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing pleasure as pain, seeing sweet as poison, that is what's going to free you from sensuality.

481
00:41:46,984 --> 00:41:48,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can engage in neutral stuff.

482
00:42:00,755 --> 00:42:03,638
[SPEAKER_02]: Inward exploration.

483
00:42:03,658 --> 00:42:06,801
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I just said before he started reading that sutta.

484
00:42:07,622 --> 00:42:09,263
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what meditation is.

485
00:42:09,484 --> 00:42:10,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Inward exploration.

486
00:42:11,005 --> 00:42:15,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Figure, like, make understanding, practicing understanding.

487
00:42:15,650 --> 00:42:19,313
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no method that will tell you how to do the understanding.

